# Task 3: Basic OPA Authorization Policies

## Objective

Install and configure Open Policy Agent (OPA) on Windows 11 and learn how to create authorization policies that determine whether a user or application is allowed to perform a specific action.

The objective is to implement policy-based authorization as part of the Post-Quantum Zero-Trust Microsegmentation Mesh project.

# What is OPA?

Open Policy Agent (OPA) is an open-source policy engine that helps applications and systems make authorization decisions based on predefined rules.

OPA uses a policy language called Rego to define and evaluate these rules.

# For example:

An administrator can access all permitted resources.
A developer can read and modify application resources.
An unauthorized user is denied access.

OPA evaluates the request against the policy and returns a decision, such as allow: true or allow: false.

# Why Are We Using OPA?

In a Zero-Trust architecture, access should not be granted automatically. Every request must be evaluated against the applicable security rules.

OPA helps implement this principle by providing centralized, policy-based authorization.

# In this project, OPA can complement Kubernetes NetworkPolicies:

Kubernetes NetworkPolicies: Control which workloads can communicate over the network.
OPA authorization policies: Evaluate whether a request or action is permitted under defined rules.
mTLS and PQC: Planned security components for workload authentication and secure communication.

These technologies provide different security controls and can work together as part of the Zero-Trust architecture.

# Environment
Operating System: Windows 11
Policy Engine: Open Policy Agent (OPA)
Policy Language: Rego
Interface: Windows Terminal / PowerShell
Project: Post-Quantum Zero-Trust Microsegmentation Mesh
Integration with Kubernetes: Planned for a later stage
